# Carom

## Overview

Carom is a neon, top-down paddle duel for the browser. Two paddles face each
{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
other across a dark field while three balls ricochet between them, off the top
and bottom walls, off a pair of obstacles in the middle of the field, and off each
other. A player scores when a ball passes the far edge behind their opponent's
paddle.
{{else}}
other across a dark field while a ball ricochets between them, off the top and
bottom walls, and off a pair of obstacles in the middle of the field. A player
scores when the ball passes the far edge behind their opponent's paddle.
{{/if}}

Carom's defining mechanic is spin: the motion of a paddle at the moment it
strikes the ball curves the ball's flight afterward. The obstacles turn the open
field into a bank-shot puzzle.

Carom has its own name, look, spin mechanic, and obstacle layout. Do not
reproduce the assets, branding, or design of any existing game.

The specification spans several files under `specs/`. Read all of them and build
the game they describe as one cohesive whole.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
## The runtime

This game stands on no engine, so the runtime under it is yours to write: the
frame loop and the delta time it measures, the canvas fit, input, audio cues,
and the debug overlay. Where this specification speaks of the runtime it means
that layer, and it states only what Carom needs from it. How the layer is
structured is yours to decide.
{{else}}
## The runtime

The game is built on the {{engine.name}} engine, which documents itself in the
`engine/` directory at the root of this repository. It owns the frame loop and
the delta time the game is given, input actions, audio cues, asset loading, the
canvas fit, and the debug overlay. Read its documentation before you start. This
specification states only what is specific to Carom.
{{/if}}

## The project you build in

The project at the root of this repository is already wired up, and its
`README.md` describes it. It is a TypeScript project built with Vite, tested with
Vitest, linted with ESLint, and formatted with Prettier. That toolchain is fixed
and supplied.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### What you implement

Everything under `src/`. The directory does not exist yet.

`index.html` loads `/src/main.ts` as its entry point, so that module is where
your build starts. Beyond that the structure is yours: the frame loop and the
canvas fit, input, audio, the diagnostics overlay, the debug and automation
surface, the figures this specification fixes, and the game itself.

### What you must not edit

- `index.html`, the page and the canvas the field is fitted into.
- The configuration: `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`,
  `eslint.config.js`, `.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`, and `.gitignore`.

`package.json` takes one kind of change: a dependency you genuinely need, added
along with the updated `package-lock.json`. Its existing entries and scripts stay
as they are.

### Every figure comes from this specification

Every number, color, key binding, cue name, and piece of screen copy this
specification fixes is stated here under a name. Name each one once in a module
of your own and read it from there, rather than restating a value at each use.
Anything this specification does not name is yours to decide.

### Your own tests

Write unit tests for what you build, beside your sources as `src/**/*.test.ts`.
`npm test` runs them in process with Vitest, in Node, with coverage measured over
`src/`. A test drives the game over its own clock for a counted number of frames,
so it needs no browser, and `@napi-rs/canvas` is installed for a test that wants
a real 2D context. Cover the parts of the game a person cannot see at a glance:
the collision resolution, the paddle bounce and spin, the serve, the scoring and
match rules, and the AI.
{{else}}
### What you implement

`src/game.ts`, and any new modules you add beside it under `src/`.

`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `Game<CaromState, CaromDebugApi>`: `initialize`
builds the state once, `update` advances it against the frame's delta time in
seconds, and `render` draws it. All three throw `"not implemented"` as they
stand. Implement them, and split the work across new files under `src/` however
suits you.

`src/game.ts` also declares and exports `CaromState` and the types it is built
from, exactly as this specification states them, and `initialize` hands the debug
surface to the engine.

### What you must not edit

- `src/main.ts`, the entry point. It stands the engine up over the page's canvas,
  binds `game` to it, and runs.
- `src/constants.ts`, holding every figure this specification fixes.
- `src/debug.ts`, holding the debug and automation surface, supplied already
  written.
- `index.html`, the page and the canvas the engine fits the field into.
- `.tcab/`, holding the vendored engine that `package.json` resolves the
  dependency from.
- `engine/`, the engine's own documentation.
- The configuration: `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`,
  `eslint.config.js`, `.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`, and `.gitignore`.

`package.json` takes one kind of change: a dependency you genuinely need, added
along with the updated `package-lock.json`. Its existing entries and scripts stay
as they are.

### Every figure comes from `src/constants.ts`

Every number, color, key binding, cue name, and piece of screen copy this
specification fixes is exported from `src/constants.ts` under the name this
specification gives it. Import it and use it. Where a spec states a value it also
names the constant that holds it, and the constant is authoritative. Anything
this specification does not name is yours to decide.

### Your own tests

Write unit tests for what you build, beside your sources as `src/**/*.test.ts`.
`npm test` runs them in process with Vitest, in Node, with coverage measured over
`src/`. A test stands the engine up over its own canvas and clock and advances the
game a counted number of frames, so it needs no browser. Cover the parts of the
game a person cannot see at a glance: the collision resolution, the paddle bounce
and spin, the serve, the scoring and match rules, and the AI.
{{/if}}

### The checks run over what you deliver

Four commands run over the finished repository, and their results are recorded
with the run:

| Command | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `npx tsc --noEmit` | Gating. Code that does not compile scores zero. |
| `npx eslint .` | Recorded. |
| `npx prettier --check .` | Recorded. |
| `npx vitest run --coverage` | Recorded, with the test count and the coverage. |

They run on the repository as you leave it, so leave it type-clean, lint-clean,
and formatted, with your tests passing. `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint`,
`npm run format`, and `npm test` run the same four locally.

### The build interface

`npm ci` followed by `npm run build` produces the complete static site into
`dist/` at the repository root, with an `index.html` at the root of that
directory as the entry point. That directory runs correctly when served as-is at
the root of any static file server. The scripts that do this are already in
`package.json`.

## Goal of this build

Produce a complete, polished, playable game that runs entirely in a browser.
This is a substantial front-end build: real-time rendered graphics, a physics
loop, multiple game states and menus, an AI opponent, and a local two-player
mode. Aim for a game a person would enjoy playing.

### Hard requirements

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Write the runtime the game stands on: the frame loop and its delta time, the
  canvas fit, input handling, audio, and the debug overlay.
{{else}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, taking the frame loop, input handling,
  audio, and debug overlay from it.
{{/if}}
- Render real graphics on the canvas. A text-only or ASCII rendering does not
  satisfy this requirement.
- Draw everything in code. The build ships no art or audio files and fetches
  nothing at runtime.
- Run in the browser with no backend. Everything needed to play is
  self-contained, and no API key or credential is needed to build, run, or play.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Hold the game's whole state in one value, the one the frame loop advances and
  the debug surface reads.
{{else}}
- Hold the game's whole state in `CaromState`.
{{/if}}
- Replace the starter `README.md` with one explaining what the game is, how to
  install dependencies, how to run it in development, how to produce the static
  production build, and the controls.

## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions, sizes, and speeds in this specification are given in the field's
own logical units on a fixed play area of `FIELD_W x FIELD_H` (`1280 x 720`,
16:9). The origin `(0, 0)` is the top-left; `x` increases to the right and `y`
increases downward. The field center is `(FIELD_CX, FIELD_CY)` (`640, 360`).

- `FIELD_W x FIELD_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the
  browser window is the runtime's: the uniform scale that preserves the aspect
  ratio, the letterboxed centering on the background color, and the device pixel
  ratio. The complete field is therefore on screen at every window size, on load
  and at any pixel density.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the runtime
  alone.
- Gameplay logic operates in logical units, independent of the rendered scale.

Every rate in this specification is per second and every duration is in seconds,
integrated against the delta time the runtime hands each update.

## Visual design

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The look is neon-on-charcoal. Every color below is fixed by this specification,
under the name the table gives it.
{{else}}
The look is neon-on-charcoal. Every color below is exported from
`src/constants.ts` on the `COLOR` object.
{{/if}}

| Element | Constant | Color |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Field background | `COLOR.bg` | `#0b0e14` |
| Raised panel fill | `COLOR.bgRaised` | `#11151f` |
| Player one paddle | `COLOR.p1` | `#3ae7c4` |
| Player two / AI paddle | `COLOR.p2` | `#ff5c8a` |
| Ball | `COLOR.ball` | `#f2f5f7` |
| Obstacles | `COLOR.obstacle` | `#ffb454` |
| Center net | `COLOR.net` | `#243044` |
| Primary text | `COLOR.text` | `#e6edf3` |
| Secondary text | `COLOR.textDim` | `#8a94a6` |
| Faint text | `COLOR.textFaint` | `#4a5567` |
| Panel border | `COLOR.panelBorder` | `#20283a` |

- Draw all text in the `MONO` font stack, a system monospace stack, so the game
  renders identically offline.
- Paddles, the ball, and obstacles have a soft neon glow, each drawn in its color
  above. The interior of each of those shapes is that exact color, so the glow is
  a halo around a solid body.
- The center net is a dashed vertical line at `x = NET_X` (`640`), drawn in
  `COLOR.net`. It is decoration and has no collision.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
- Every ball leaves its own motion trail. Behind a moving ball, draw a fading
  tail that traces its recent path, so it visibly curves whenever spin curves the
  flight. It reads as one continuous streak, a smooth comet, and tapers from the
  ball: widest and brightest where it meets the ball, then narrowing and fading
  smoothly to nothing at its oldest end. A trail represents `TRAIL_TIME` (`0.13`)
  seconds of recent travel, so its length is proportional to that ball's current
  speed. It stretches as the ball speeds up, shortens as it slows, and collapses
  to almost nothing while the ball waits at its home point. Draw it in
  `COLOR.ball`.
{{else}}
- The ball leaves a motion trail. Behind the moving ball, draw a fading tail that
  traces its recent path, so it visibly curves whenever spin curves the flight.
  It reads as one continuous streak, a smooth comet, and tapers from the ball:
  widest and brightest where it meets the ball, then narrowing and fading
  smoothly to nothing at its oldest end. The trail represents `TRAIL_TIME`
  (`0.13`) seconds of recent travel, so its length is proportional to the ball's
  current speed. It stretches as the ball speeds up, shortens as it slows, and
  collapses to almost nothing during the pre-serve hold while the ball is still.
  Draw it in `COLOR.ball`.
{{/if}}
- The title screen, the in-match view, and the match-over screen each carry this
  palette and type.
